export const MOLECULES = [
  // History-changers & life-savers
  { id: "urea", name: "Urea", formula: "CH4N2O", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Urea is the nitrogen waste in urine. Making it from inorganic salts showed that an organic molecule did not need a living body.", world: "A clinical chemist measures urea in blood to see how well the kidneys are clearing nitrogen waste. A farmer meets urea as a nitrogen fertilizer. The historical lesson for a chemist is that this molecule from a living body can also be made from inorganic salts." },
  { id: "benzene", name: "Benzene", formula: "C6H6", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Benzene is a ring of six carbons and six hydrogens. That ring shows up in dyes, fuels, and many medicines.", note: "All six carbon–carbon bonds in this model are the same length, 1.395 Å.", world: "A refinery engineer tracks benzene in fuel because it is part of gasoline and a workplace hazard. An industrial chemist uses the six-carbon ring as a starting piece for plastics, dyes, and other products. The ring itself is the pattern those jobs keep recognizing." },
  { id: "penicillin-g", name: "Penicillin G", formula: "C16H18N2O4S", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Penicillin G is an antibiotic. Its use opened the antibiotic age.", note: "This is the natural shape.", world: "A physician prescribes penicillin G for infections that are still sensitive to it. A pharmacist dispenses it and checks that the person is not allergic. A medicinal chemist uses the beta-lactam ring, the square of atoms in this molecule, as the pattern behind many later antibiotics." },
  { id: "aspirin", name: "Acetylsalicylic acid", formula: "C9H8O4", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Aspirin is a pain reliever and a fever reducer. This drawing is acetylsalicylic acid.", world: "A pharmacist dispenses aspirin for pain and fever. A cardiologist also uses a low dose for some people, to lower the chance of a clot. The molecule on this page is the one in the tablet, acetylsalicylic acid." },
  { id: "paracetamol", name: "Paracetamol", formula: "C8H9NO2", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Paracetamol, also called acetaminophen, is a common pain reliever and fever reducer.", world: "A pharmacist checks a paracetamol dose against a person's age and weight, because too much can injure the liver. Pediatricians and emergency physicians use that same check when someone has taken more than the label allows. The medicine is also called acetaminophen." },
  { id: "ibuprofen", name: "Ibuprofen", formula: "C13H18O2", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Ibuprofen is a pain reliever. This model is the S form, the mirror image that does the work. Many tablets contain both mirror images.", world: "A pharmacist checks an ibuprofen dose and asks about the stomach, the kidneys, and other medicines. A physician uses it for pain, fever, and inflammation. Many tablets contain both mirror images, and the S form is the one that does the work." },
  { id: "dna", name: "DNA", formula: "", formulaLabel: "double helix", category: "history", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "DNA stores genetic instructions as a double helix. It is a polymer of four bases: adenine, thymine, guanine, and cytosine.", facts: [["What it is", "Two sugar-phosphate strands wound together, with four bases paired in the middle."], ["Where it shows up", "Cell nuclei, mitochondria, and many viruses."], ["Why it matters", "The order of the bases is the cell's record of which proteins to build."]], world: "A genetic counselor reads a DNA test with a family when a diagnosis depends on the sequence. A forensic scientist compares DNA from a scene with a person's sample. A physician orders that kind of test. The order of the bases is what each of those jobs is reading." },
  { id: "glucose", name: "Glucose", formula: "C6H12O6", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Glucose is the sugar your cells use for fuel. This model is β-D-glucose in the chair shape.", world: "A nurse measures blood glucose, and an endocrinologist uses the number to adjust insulin or another treatment. A food scientist counts glucose when a label lists sugars. Cells use this sugar as fuel, which is why the number matters in a clinic." },
  { id: "sucrose", name: "Sucrose", formula: "C12H22O11", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Sucrose is table sugar. One half is glucose and the other half is fructose.", note: "The six-membered ring is the glucose half. The five-membered ring is the fructose half.", world: "A baker and a food scientist use sucrose for sweetness and for texture in ordinary food. A dentist talks about it because bacteria on teeth turn sugar into acid. One half of the molecule is glucose and the other half is fructose." },
  { id: "quinine", name: "Quinine", formula: "C20H24N2O2", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Quinine is a malaria medicine first taken from cinchona bark.", note: "This is quinine, not quinidine.", world: "A physician may use quinine for malaria when a newer drug is not the right choice. A flavor chemist knows a very small amount of it as the bitter note in tonic water. The molecule was first taken from cinchona bark." },
  { id: "morphine", name: "Morphine", formula: "C17H19NO3", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Morphine is a powerful pain medicine from the opium poppy. It is tightly controlled.", world: "A physician in a hospital or a hospice prescribes morphine for severe pain. A pharmacist dispenses it under strict rules, and a nurse records the dose. It comes from the opium poppy and it is tightly controlled." },
  { id: "insulin", name: "Insulin", formula: "", formulaLabel: "protein", category: "history", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Insulin is a protein hormone. People with type 1 diabetes take a medical form of it.", facts: [["What it is", "A protein hormone of two chains linked by sulfur bridges."], ["Where it shows up", "Made in the pancreas."], ["Why it matters", "It lets cells take in glucose. People with type 1 diabetes take a medical form of it."]], world: "An endocrinologist prescribes insulin for type 1 diabetes and for some people with type 2. A nurse or a pharmacist teaches how the prescribed dose matches meals and blood glucose. The hormone lets cells take sugar in." },
  { id: "ethinylestradiol", name: "Ethinylestradiol", formula: "C20H24O2", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Ethinylestradiol is a synthetic estrogen used in many birth-control pills.", note: "The ethynyl and the OH on the same carbon point opposite ways, the form used in the medicine.", world: "A physician and a pharmacist meet ethinylestradiol in many birth-control pills, usually together with a second hormone. They check a person's medical history, because the medicine is not right for everyone. It is a synthetic estrogen." },
  { id: "teflon", name: "Polytetrafluoroethylene", formula: "C6F14", formulaLabel: "C6F14 piece", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Teflon is polytetrafluoroethylene, a slippery plastic of CF2 units. This model is six carbons of that chain, fluorine on every bond, including the ends.", note: "The plastic does not stop at a CF3.", world: "A materials engineer chooses this plastic when a surface must be slippery or must resist harsh chemicals. A cook meets it as the coating on some nonstick pans. A laboratory chemist uses it in tape and in vessels. The real material is a long chain, and this model is only a short piece." },
  { id: "paclitaxel", name: "Paclitaxel", formula: "C47H51NO14", category: "history", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Paclitaxel, also called taxol, is a chemotherapy medicine first found in yew bark.", world: "An oncologist prescribes paclitaxel for several cancers. A pharmacist and a nurse give it as an infusion in a clinic. The medicine was first found in yew bark, and it is also called taxol." },

  // Molecular wonders & strange shapes
  { id: "buckminsterfullerene", name: "Buckminsterfullerene", formula: "C60", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Buckminsterfullerene is sixty carbons in a soccer-ball cage: twelve pentagons and twenty hexagons.", note: "Shorter bonds sit between two hexagons (1.391 Å). Longer bonds sit on the pentagons (1.455 Å).", world: "A materials scientist studies C60 as a closed cage of sixty carbons. Researchers have used related cages in work on electronics and solar cells. The twelve pentagons and twenty hexagons are what make the soccer-ball shape those jobs care about." },
  { id: "cubane", name: "Cubane", formula: "C8H8", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Cubane is eight CH units on the corners of a cube, so the carbon angles are 90° instead of tetrahedral.", world: "A physical organic chemist studies cubane because the carbon angles are 90 degrees, far from the usual tetrahedron. A medicinal chemist has also studied the cube as a rigid shape in research molecules. It is a laboratory cage, not a product on a store shelf." },
  { id: "prismane", name: "Prismane", formula: "C6H6", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Prismane is a triangular prism of six CH units, a strained isomer of benzene.", world: "A physical organic chemist studies prismane because it is a strained isomer of benzene, the same formula in a very different shape. The job is to see how far a carbon cage can be bent and still hold together. It is not an industrial chemical." },
  { id: "olympiadane", name: "Olympiadane", formula: "C216H228F72N12O30P12", category: "wonders", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Olympiadane is five molecular rings interlocked in a chain, named for the Olympic rings.", facts: [["What it is", "Five molecular rings threaded into one chain, like the Olympic rings."], ["Where it shows up", "Made in a laboratory as a demonstration of interlocking rings."], ["Why it matters", "The rings are trapped through one another. They are not held by a bond you can point to between the rings."]], world: "A supramolecular chemist made olympiadane to show that five molecular rings can be interlocked on purpose. The rings stay together because they are threaded, not because one bond joins ring to ring. It is a laboratory demonstration, not a factory material." },
  { id: "nanokid", name: "NanoKid", formula: "C39H42O2", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "NanoKid is a real molecule drawn to look like a person, with oxygen atoms for the eyes.", world: "A chemistry teacher uses NanoKid to show that a real molecule can be drawn to look like a person and still be a genuine structure. The oxygen atoms are the eyes. It came from a research group as a teaching molecule, not as a drug or a plastic." },
  { id: "bullvalene", name: "Bullvalene", formula: "C10H10", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Bullvalene, C10H10, keeps rearranging at room temperature so the carbons trade places.", note: "One frozen shape of the cage. At room temperature the carbons trade places.", world: "A physical organic chemist uses bullvalene to teach a molecule that keeps rearranging at room temperature. The carbons trade places, so a measurement can show one signal where a frozen shape would show several. This model is one frozen shape of that cage." },
  { id: "fenestrane", name: "Fenestrane", formula: "C9H12", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "The windowpane molecule here is [4.4.4.4]fenestrane, C9H12, a flattened carbon center with four fused rings.", note: "The carbon in the middle sits almost in the plane of its four neighbors.", world: "A physical organic chemist studies fenestrane when the question is how flat a carbon atom can be pushed. The central carbon in this windowpane cage sits almost in a plane. It is a laboratory hydrocarbon, not an industrial chemical." },
  { id: "pagodane", name: "Pagodane", formula: "C20H20", category: "wonders", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Pagodane is a cage hydrocarbon, C20H20, named because the shape stacks like a pagoda.", facts: [["What it is", "A cage of twenty CH units, stacked in a shape that suggested a pagoda."], ["Where it shows up", "A laboratory hydrocarbon."], ["Why it matters", "The cage is a rigid carbon framework, useful when a lesson is about a strained shape."]], world: "A physical organic chemist studies pagodane as a rigid cage of twenty CH units. The shape suggested a pagoda, which is how it got the name. It is a laboratory hydrocarbon, useful when the lesson is a strained carbon framework." },
  { id: "housane", name: "Housane", formula: "C5H8", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Housane is bicyclo[2.1.0]pentane. The shape is a square of carbons with a triangular roof.", world: "A physical organic chemist studies housane as a small strained ring, a square of carbons with a triangular roof. The house shape is the lesson. It is not a product in a catalog of industrial chemicals." },
  { id: "nanotube", name: "Carbon nanotube", formula: "C40H20", category: "wonders", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "This is a short (5,5) nanotube: forty carbons in hexagons, rolled, with hydrogen on the open ends.", note: "Each carbon–carbon link is the graphite length, about 1.42 Å. The straight-line distance in this rolled model is 1.41–1.42 Å.", world: "A materials engineer uses carbon nanotubes in some strong, light composites and in conductive films. An electronics researcher cares how the sheet is rolled, because that choice changes the tube's electrical behavior. This model is a short capped piece, not an endless tube." },

  // Sensory explosions
  { id: "capsaicin", name: "Capsaicin", formula: "C18H27NO3", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Capsaicin is the molecule that makes a chili feel hot. It trips a heat sensor; it is not a flame.", note: "The chain has a trans double bond.", world: "A flavor chemist doses capsaicin when a food should taste hot. A physician uses a capsaicin patch or cream for some nerve pain, because the molecule that burns the tongue can, in a medicine, quiet a pain signal. It is the hot molecule in a chili, not a flame." },
  { id: "miraculin", name: "Miraculin", formula: "", formulaLabel: "protein", category: "sensory", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Miraculin is a protein in miracle fruit that makes sour foods taste sweet for a while.", facts: [["What it is", "A protein in the berry called miracle fruit."], ["Where it shows up", "The fruit of Synsepalum dulcificum, in West Africa."], ["Why it matters", "After it, a lemon can taste sweet for a while. The protein binds a sweet receptor."]], world: "A food scientist studies miraculin because, after it, a lemon can taste sweet for a while. The protein comes from miracle fruit, and it is not the same thing as adding sugar. It binds a sweet receptor on the tongue." },
  { id: "thaumatin", name: "Thaumatin", formula: "", formulaLabel: "protein", category: "sensory", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Thaumatin is a very sweet protein from a West African fruit.", facts: [["What it is", "A small protein that tastes very sweet."], ["Where it shows up", "The fruit of Thaumatococcus daniellii, a West African plant."], ["Why it matters", "By weight it is far sweeter than table sugar, and it is a protein, not a sugar."]], world: "A flavor chemist uses thaumatin as a sweetener and a flavor modifier in some foods. By weight it is far sweeter than table sugar, and it is a protein, not a sugar. It comes from a West African fruit." },
  { id: "geosmin", name: "Geosmin", formula: "C12H22O", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Geosmin is the molecule that makes rain on dry soil smell the way it does.", note: "This is the form soil bacteria make.", world: "A water-quality chemist measures geosmin when drinking water smells like damp soil. A winemaker meets the same molecule: a little can smell like earth, and too much can spoil a wine. Soil bacteria make it." },
  { id: "thioacetone", name: "Thioacetone", formula: "C3H6S", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Thioacetone is famous for a smell so strong that a little of it empties a room.", world: "A laboratory safety officer cites thioacetone as a sulfur smell strong enough to clear a room. Chemists who have worked near it treat a small release as a serious nuisance. It is a cautionary laboratory chemical, not a fragrance anyone bottles on purpose." },
  { id: "vanillin", name: "Vanillin", formula: "C8H8O3", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Vanillin is the main molecule in the smell of vanilla.", world: "A flavor chemist uses vanillin when a food, a perfume, or a candle should smell like vanilla. Most of the vanillin in those products is made for the industry rather than taken from a vanilla bean. The chemist still has to match the smell people expect from the bean." },
  { id: "menthol", name: "Menthol", formula: "C10H20O", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Menthol is the cooling molecule in mint. This model is (−)-menthol in the chair shape.", world: "A flavor chemist uses menthol for a mint taste and a cooling feel. A pharmacist meets it in cough drops and in creams that feel cold on the skin. This model is the form found in mint." },
  { id: "trimethylamine", name: "Trimethylamine", formula: "C3H9N", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Trimethylamine smells like old fish. Bodies make a little of it, and bacteria make more.", note: "Nitrogen sits at the top of a shallow pyramid. Each carbon–nitrogen–carbon angle in this model is 110.5°.", world: "A food scientist tracks trimethylamine as a sign that fish is no longer fresh. A physician meets the same smell in a rare condition where the body cannot clear the molecule. Bacteria make it as well, which is why old fish smells the way it does." },
  { id: "cadaverine", name: "Cadaverine", formula: "C5H14N2", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Cadaverine is one of the molecules in the smell of decay. It is a five-carbon diamine.", world: "A forensic scientist and a food scientist meet cadaverine as one of the amines bacteria make when protein breaks down. It is a clue to decay. Nobody wants it in a kitchen, and it is not added to food on purpose." },
  { id: "isoamyl-acetate", name: "Isoamyl acetate", formula: "C7H14O2", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Isoamyl acetate smells like banana. It is the ester in some candies and in a ripe banana.", world: "A flavor chemist uses isoamyl acetate for a banana smell in candy and drinks. A laboratory chemist also meets it as a solvent nicknamed banana oil. A ripe banana makes the same ester." },
  { id: "acetic-acid", name: "Acetic acid", formula: "C2H4O2", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Acetic acid is the sour molecule in vinegar.", world: "A food producer uses acetic acid, diluted, as vinegar. A chemical engineer meets the concentrated acid as a large-scale industrial chemical. The sour taste in the kitchen and the tank in the plant are the same molecule at very different strengths." },
  { id: "citric-acid", name: "Citric acid", formula: "C6H8O7", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Citric acid is the sour molecule in lemons. Cells also use it while they break food down for energy.", world: "A food scientist uses citric acid to make a drink sour and to keep cut fruit from browning. A biochemist teaches the citric acid cycle, the path cells use while they release energy from food. Lemons are the familiar source." },
  { id: "limonene", name: "(R)-Limonene", formula: "C10H16", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "This is (R)-limonene, the form in orange peel. Its mirror image smells more like pine or lemon.", world: "A flavor chemist uses this form of limonene for an orange smell. A formulator of cleaning products uses limonene from citrus peel as a solvent, and still treats the concentrated oil as something that can irritate skin. The mirror image smells different." },
  { id: "carvone-r", name: "(R)-Carvone", formula: "C10H14O", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "This is (R)-carvone. It smells like spearmint. Its mirror image, (S)-carvone, smells like caraway.", world: "A flavor chemist uses (R)-carvone for spearmint in gum and toothpaste. The mirror image smells like caraway, so the chemist has to know which one is in the bottle. The formula is the same. The shape is what the nose notices." },
  { id: "carvone-s", name: "(S)-Carvone", formula: "C10H14O", category: "sensory", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "This is (S)-carvone. It smells like caraway. It is the mirror image of the spearmint form.", world: "A flavor chemist uses (S)-carvone for the smell of caraway in rye bread and in some spirits. It is the mirror image of the spearmint form. A nose, and a flavor lab, can tell them apart even though the formula matches." },

  // High energy & explosives — cards only
  { id: "tnt", name: "TNT", formula: "C7H5N3O6", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "TNT is a solid explosive that shows up in lessons about demolition and war.", world: "Bomb-disposal technicians and military engineers are the people who deal with TNT in munitions and in unexploded ordnance. Mining engineers have also met it in demolition work. The public fact is the job and the hazard." },
  { id: "nitroglycerin", name: "Nitroglycerin", formula: "C3H5N3O9", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Nitroglycerin is both an old heart medicine and a powerful explosive.", world: "A cardiologist and an emergency physician use a medical form of nitroglycerin for chest pain from the heart. Bomb-disposal technicians treat the explosive form as a material only trained people handle. The two uses are the same molecule in very different settings." },
  { id: "azidoazide", name: "Azidoazide azide", formula: "C2N14", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Azidoazide azide is a nitrogen-rich molecule, formula C2N14, so sensitive that shock can set it off.", world: "Energetic-materials researchers have studied azidoazide azide because a small shock can set it off. It is not a mining product and it is not a medicine. The public fact is the sensitivity, not a method." },
  { id: "octanitrocubane", name: "Octanitrocubane", formula: "C8N8O16", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Octanitrocubane is cubane with a nitro group on every carbon, studied as a high-energy material.", world: "Energetic-materials researchers study octanitrocubane as a high-energy cage. It is a laboratory material. A mining engineer does not order it as an ordinary charge." },
  { id: "hmx", name: "HMX", formula: "C4H8N8O8", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "HMX, also called octogen, is a powerful explosive used in military charges.", world: "Military ordnance technicians handle HMX inside regulated charges and some rocket propellants. It is also called octogen. It is not a classroom chemical." },
  { id: "cl-20", name: "CL-20", formula: "C6H6N12O12", category: "energy", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "CL-20 is a modern high-energy explosive studied for propellants and charges.", facts: [["What it is", "A cage of carbon and nitrogen with a nitro group on each nitrogen. Formula C6H6N12O12."], ["Where it shows up", "Research on propellants and charges."], ["Why it matters", "It is a modern high-energy explosive. This page stays with that public fact."]], world: "Propulsion researchers study CL-20 as a high-energy material for charges and propellants. It stays in specialized laboratories. This page keeps to that public fact." },
  { id: "ammonium-nitrate", name: "Ammonium nitrate", formula: "N2H4O3", formulaLabel: "NH4NO3", category: "energy", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Ammonium nitrate is a fertilizer salt that can explode if it is stored or heated wrongly.", facts: [["What it is", "A salt of ammonium and nitrate, NH4NO3."], ["Where it shows up", "Fertilizer, and some cold packs."], ["Why it matters", "It feeds crops. A large pile can detonate if it is heated or shocked."]], world: "A farmer uses ammonium nitrate as a nitrogen fertilizer. A mining engineer meets it in industrial blasting agents. Fire-safety officials treat a large store of it as a serious hazard, because a pile can detonate if it is heated or badly kept." },
  { id: "tatp", name: "Acetone peroxide", formula: "C9H18O6", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "TATP is an unstable explosive that has been used in attacks. Making it is a crime.", world: "Bomb-disposal technicians and forensic chemists are the people who meet TATP, because it has been used in attacks. Making it is a crime. This page does not say how." },
  { id: "clf3", name: "Chlorine trifluoride", formula: "ClF3", category: "energy", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Chlorine trifluoride can set fire to materials that do not usually burn.", facts: [["What it is", "Chlorine with three fluorines, a T-shaped molecule."], ["Where it shows up", "Industrial fluorination, in special containers."], ["Why it matters", "It can set fire to materials that do not usually burn."]], world: "A process engineer in a semiconductor plant meets chlorine trifluoride as a gas used inside closed equipment to clean coating chambers. Specialists keep it in special containers, because it can set fire to materials that do not usually burn. It is not a school chemical." },
  { id: "foof", name: "Dioxygen difluoride", formula: "O2F2", category: "energy", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Dioxygen difluoride, FOOF, reacts violently with most things it touches, even when very cold.", world: "Inorganic chemists have studied dioxygen difluoride in cold, specialized laboratories, because it reacts with almost anything it touches. It is not an industrial product and it is not a medicine. The public fact is how violently it reacts, even when the sample is very cold." },

  // Chemical extremes — cards only
  { id: "botulinum", name: "Botulinum toxin", formula: "", formulaLabel: "protein", category: "extremes", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Botulinum toxin is a protein from a bacterium, among the most poisonous substances known. A medical form is used in tiny controlled doses.", facts: [["What it is", "A protein from the bacterium Clostridium botulinum."], ["Where it shows up", "Poorly preserved food, and, in a medical form, in controlled doses."], ["Why it matters", "It is among the most poisonous substances known. No dose is given on this page."]], world: "A neurologist uses a licensed medical form of botulinum toxin for some muscle spasms and for chronic migraine. A food-safety inspector worries about the same protein when preserved food has been kept badly. No dose is given on this page." },
  { id: "fluoroantimonic-acid", name: "Fluoroantimonic acid", formula: "HSbF6", category: "extremes", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Fluoroantimonic acid is among the strongest acids known.", facts: [["What it is", "A fluoroantimonate acid, among the strongest acids known."], ["Where it shows up", "Superacid research laboratories, not a school bench."], ["Why it matters", "It protonates molecules that ordinary acids leave alone."]], world: "A research chemist meets fluoroantimonic acid only in a superacid laboratory, where the question is how it protonates molecules that ordinary acids leave alone. It is not a school acid. The bottle, if a lab has one, stays in that specialized room." },
  { id: "vx", name: "VX", formula: "C11H26NO2PS", category: "extremes", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "VX is a nerve agent, illegal to make or keep. It stops nerves from turning a signal off.", note: "This model is the S form at phosphorus.", world: "Chemical-defense specialists, toxicologists, and emergency physicians study VX so they can recognize a nerve-agent exposure and treat it. Making or keeping it is illegal. This page does not say how it is made." },
  { id: "maitotoxin", name: "Maitotoxin", formula: "C164H256Na2O68S2", formulaLabel: "C164H256O68S2Na2", category: "extremes", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Maitotoxin is a huge toxin made by some algae and concentrated in certain fish.", facts: [["What it is", "A very large ladder-shaped toxin, formula C164H256O68S2Na2."], ["Where it shows up", "Some marine algae, and fish that have eaten them."], ["Why it matters", "It is one of the largest toxins that is not a protein, and it disturbs calcium in cells."]], world: "A marine chemist studies maitotoxin as a toxin made by some algae and concentrated in certain fish. A toxicologist cares because it disturbs calcium in cells. It is not a material a hospital stocks." },
  { id: "batrachotoxin", name: "Batrachotoxin", formula: "C31H42N2O6", category: "extremes", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Batrachotoxin is a poison from some frogs and beetles. It holds nerve channels open.", world: "Neuroscientists have used batrachotoxin in the laboratory as a tool that holds a nerve channel open. Toxicologists treat it as a poison from some frogs and beetles, not as a medicine. It is not something a person collects." },
  { id: "fluoxetine", name: "Fluoxetine", formula: "C17H18F3NO", category: "extremes", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Fluoxetine, sold as Prozac, is a medicine that changes serotonin signaling.", note: "This model is the R form. The medicine is sold as both mirror images.", world: "A psychiatrist prescribes fluoxetine, sold as Prozac, for depression and for some other conditions. A pharmacist checks the dose and the other medicines, because a drug that changes serotonin signaling can interact. The medicine is sold as both mirror images." },
  { id: "carbon-monoxide", name: "Carbon monoxide", formula: "CO", category: "extremes", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Carbon monoxide is a colorless gas. It binds to hemoglobin more tightly than oxygen does.", note: "The link is a triple bond.", world: "A firefighter and an emergency physician treat carbon monoxide as a silent poison from incomplete burning. A steelmaker also meets it as a gas in a furnace, which is why detectors and ventilation matter in that plant. It binds to hemoglobin more tightly than oxygen does." },
  { id: "dimethylmercury", name: "Dimethylmercury", formula: "C2H6Hg", category: "extremes", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Dimethylmercury is a liquid mercury compound that passes through skin and is extremely poisonous.", facts: [["What it is", "A liquid with two methyl groups on mercury."], ["Where it shows up", "A laboratory mercury compound, not a household chemical."], ["Why it matters", "It passes through skin, including some gloves, and it is extremely poisonous."]], world: "Toxicologists and laboratory safety officers treat dimethylmercury as a liquid that can pass through skin, including some gloves. It is a research hazard, not a school chemical, and not a household product. The public fact is the danger of contact." },
  { id: "cyanide", name: "Hydrogen cyanide", formula: "CHN", formulaLabel: "HCN", category: "extremes", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Hydrogen cyanide is a fast poison. It blocks cells from using oxygen.", note: "Hydrogen sits on the carbon. The carbon–nitrogen link is a triple bond.", world: "Emergency physicians and poison-control specialists treat hydrogen cyanide as a fast poison that blocks cells from using oxygen. Industrial hygienists also meet cyanide in some mining and metal-finishing workplaces, which is why those sites have strict rules. This page does not describe those processes." },
  { id: "polonium-hexafluoride", name: "Polonium hexafluoride", formula: "F6Po", formulaLabel: "PoF6", category: "extremes", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Polonium hexafluoride is written PoF6. Polonium is radioactive, and this is not a classroom chemical.", facts: [["What it is", "A compound written PoF6. Polonium is radioactive."], ["Where it shows up", "The literature of radioactive fluorides, not a classroom shelf."], ["Why it matters", "It is an extreme on this list. The public fact is the formula and the radioactivity."]], world: "Nuclear chemists meet polonium compounds only in specialized radiological laboratories. PoF6 is a formula in that literature, not a material a school or a hospital stocks. Polonium is radioactive, which is the public fact that matters here." },

  // Mind-alterers & neurotransmitters
  { id: "dopamine", name: "Dopamine", formula: "C8H11NO2", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Dopamine is a neurotransmitter tied to reward, movement, and attention.", note: "This model is the neutral molecule. In the body the nitrogen is usually charged.", world: "A neurologist talks about dopamine when a movement disorder is the problem, and usually prescribes a related medicine rather than dopamine itself, because a tablet of dopamine does not reach the brain well. A neuroscientist measures it as a signal between nerve cells. Reward, movement, and attention are the lessons tied to that signal." },
  { id: "adrenaline", name: "Adrenaline", formula: "C9H13NO3", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Adrenaline, also called epinephrine, is the hormone of a sudden scare. This is the R form the body makes.", note: "This model is the neutral molecule. In the body the nitrogen is usually charged.", world: "A paramedic and an emergency physician give epinephrine for anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction. An anesthesiologist also meets it as a medicine that tightens blood vessels. It is the hormone of a sudden scare, and the medicine in an emergency kit." },
  { id: "l-dopa", name: "L-DOPA", formula: "C9H11NO4", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "L-DOPA is the amino acid the body turns into dopamine. This is the L form used as a medicine.", note: "This model is the neutral acid. In water the nitrogen is usually charged.", world: "A neurologist prescribes L-DOPA for Parkinson's disease, because the brain can turn it into dopamine. A pharmacist watches the timing of the doses, because the effect can rise and fall through the day. This is the L form used as that medicine." },
  { id: "histamine", name: "Histamine", formula: "C5H9N3", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Histamine is the signal behind a hive, a sneeze, and a lot of stomach acid.", note: "This model is the neutral molecule. In the body the side-chain nitrogen is usually charged.", world: "An allergist explains histamine when a person has hives or a sneeze, and a pharmacist dispenses an antihistamine that blocks the signal. A gastroenterologist also meets histamine as one signal that tells the stomach to make acid. The same molecule sits behind a hive and a lot of stomach acid." },
  { id: "serotonin", name: "Serotonin", formula: "C10H12N2O", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Serotonin is a neurotransmitter that lessons connect with mood, sleep, and digestion.", note: "This model is the neutral molecule. In the body the side-chain nitrogen is usually charged.", world: "A psychiatrist talks about serotonin when a medicine for mood, sleep, or anxiety is on the table. Most of those medicines do not hand the body a dose of serotonin. They change how nerve cells handle the serotonin they already make." },
  { id: "caffeine", name: "Caffeine", formula: "C8H10N4O2", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Caffeine is the molecule in coffee and tea that keeps you alert.", note: "The rings are aromatic.", world: "A food scientist sets the caffeine in coffee, tea, and soft drinks. A physician asks about it when a person cannot sleep, has a racing heart, or is pregnant, because the molecule that keeps someone alert is also a drug. The rings in the model are the ones in the cup." },
  { id: "lsd", name: "LSD", formula: "C20H25N3O", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "LSD is a synthetic molecule that changes perception. Possessing it without authority is illegal.", note: "This is the (+) form.", world: "Forensic chemists identify LSD when an investigation requires it. Clinical researchers have studied it only in approved trials. Possessing it without authority is illegal, and this page does not describe a use." },
  { id: "psilocybin", name: "Psilocybin", formula: "C12H17N2O4P", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Psilocybin is the molecule in some mushrooms that changes perception.", world: "Clinical researchers study psilocybin in approved trials, including trials about depression. It is not an ordinary medicine on a pharmacy shelf. Forensic chemists identify it when the law requires that." },
  { id: "thc", name: "THC", formula: "C21H30O2", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "THC is the main molecule in cannabis that changes mood and perception.", note: "This is (−)-trans-Δ9-tetrahydrocannabinol, the main form in the plant.", world: "Pharmacists and physicians, in places that allow medical cannabis, meet THC in regulated products. Forensic chemists identify it when the law asks them to. It is the main molecule in cannabis that changes mood and perception." },
  { id: "nicotine", name: "Nicotine", formula: "C10H14N2", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Nicotine is the addictive molecule in tobacco. It acts on nerve receptors.", note: "This is (S)-nicotine, the form in the tobacco plant.", world: "Physicians and pharmacists meet nicotine in patches, gum, and lozenges that help people stop smoking. Public-health workers track it because tobacco addiction is widespread. It acts on nerve receptors, which is why those products exist." },
  { id: "oxytocin", name: "Oxytocin", formula: "C43H66N12O12S2", category: "mind", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Oxytocin is a small peptide hormone tied to birth, bonding, and milk release.", facts: [["What it is", "A peptide hormone of nine amino acids, closed with a sulfur bridge."], ["Where it shows up", "Made in the hypothalamus and released from the pituitary."], ["Why it matters", "Lessons connect it with birth, milk release, and bonding."]], world: "An obstetrician or a midwife may give a medical form of oxytocin to start or strengthen labor, or to help the uterus contract after birth. A nurse records that dose. The hospital job is the contraction, not a story about personality." },
  { id: "endorphins", name: "Endorphins", formula: "", formulaLabel: "peptides", category: "mind", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Endorphins are a family of peptides the body uses to dull pain.", facts: [["What it is", "A family of peptides, not one molecule. The members differ in length."], ["Where it shows up", "Made in the brain and in other tissues."], ["Why it matters", "The body uses them when it dulls pain."]], world: "A physician explains endorphins as peptides the body itself uses to dull pain. An exercise scientist is careful with the claim that a run floods the blood with them: the peptides are real, and the size of that effect is still studied. They are a family, not one molecule." },
  { id: "gaba", name: "GABA", formula: "C4H9NO2", category: "mind", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "GABA is the main calming neurotransmitter in the brain. The name is gamma-aminobutyric acid.", note: "This model is the neutral acid. In water the nitrogen is usually charged and the acid has given up its hydrogen.", world: "A neurologist talks about GABA as the brain's main calming signal. A pharmacist meets medicines for seizures and for anxiety that act on the receptors GABA uses, rather than on a bottle of GABA itself. The name is gamma-aminobutyric acid." },

  // Materials
  { id: "graphene", name: "Graphene", formula: "C24H12", formulaLabel: "C24H12 piece", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Graphene is a sheet of carbon hexagons, one atom thick. This model is coronene: seven of those hexagons, with hydrogen on the rim.", note: "The sheet itself has no rim. This piece stops.", world: "A materials scientist studies graphene for thin, strong, conductive films. An electronics engineer cares that the sheet is one atom thick and conducts, and still has to turn a laboratory flake into something a factory can make. This model is seven hexagons with hydrogen on the rim, not the endless sheet." },
  { id: "kevlar", name: "Kevlar", formula: "C20H18N4O2", formulaLabel: "C20H18N4O2 piece", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Kevlar is a strong fiber of benzene rings joined by amide links. This model is a short capped piece: three rings, two amides, and NH2 where the fiber would continue.", note: "It is not the whole fiber.", world: "A polymer engineer works with Kevlar when a fiber has to be strong and light, as in body armor, some ropes, and protective gear. The amide links and the way the chains pack are what make the fiber hard to pull apart. This model is a short capped piece of that pattern, not the whole fiber." },
  { id: "nylon-66", name: "Nylon 6,6", formula: "C10H20N2O2", formulaLabel: "C10H20N2O2 piece", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Nylon 6,6 is a fiber of a six-carbon diamine and a six-carbon diacid. This model is that diamine with an acetyl cap on each nitrogen.", note: "The fiber also repeats a four-carbon diacid between the amides. This piece is not the whole fiber.", world: "A polymer engineer chooses nylon 6,6 for clothing fiber, carpet, and tough plastic parts. The repeat of a six-carbon diamine and a six-carbon diacid is what the engineer is specifying. This model is only the diamine piece with caps, not the whole fiber." },
  { id: "spidroin", name: "Spider silk", formula: "", formulaLabel: "protein", category: "materials", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Spider silk is a protein fiber. The strength is in how the chains pack.", facts: [["What it is", "The protein a spider spins into silk. Different glands make different silks."], ["Where it shows up", "Spider silk glands."], ["Why it matters", "Strength comes from how the chains pack: crystalline pieces in a softer matrix."]], world: "A materials scientist studies spider silk because the protein fiber is strong and stretchy at once. A bioengineer tries to make related proteins in other organisms, because farming spiders for silk does not scale. The strength is in how the chains pack." },
  { id: "mbba", name: "MBBA", formula: "C18H21NO", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "MBBA is a liquid crystal: a rod-shaped molecule that lines up and still flows.", note: "The carbon–nitrogen double bond is trans, which makes the rod.", world: "A display researcher studies MBBA as an early liquid crystal that lines up and still flows near room temperature. Modern screens use other liquid crystals. MBBA remains a teaching example of the rod shape a display chemist wants." },
  { id: "barium-titanate", name: "Barium titanate", formula: "BaO3Ti", formulaLabel: "BaTiO3", category: "materials", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Barium titanate, BaTiO3, is a ceramic that stores electric charge.", facts: [["What it is", "A ceramic crystal, BaTiO3: barium, titanium, and oxygen in a repeating cell."], ["Where it shows up", "Capacitors, and some ultrasound transducers."], ["Why it matters", "The crystal stores electric charge, which is why it shows up in electronics."]], world: "An electrical engineer specifies barium titanate in capacitors, because the ceramic stores electric charge. An ultrasound engineer also meets it in transducers that turn an electric signal into vibration. The repeating cell is BaTiO3." },
  { id: "perovskite", name: "Perovskite", formula: "", formulaLabel: "CH3NH3PbX3", category: "materials", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "This perovskite is a solar-cell crystal: a methylammonium ion, lead, and a halide (the X).", facts: [["What it is", "A crystal family. The solar-cell ones pair a methylammonium ion, lead, and a halide."], ["Where it shows up", "Research solar cells."], ["Why it matters", "The crystal absorbs light and moves charge. Which halide sits in the X changes the color."]], world: "A solar-cell researcher studies this perovskite because the crystal absorbs light and moves charge. The engineer still has to solve stability: water and heat can wreck a cell that looks excellent when it is fresh. Which halide sits in the crystal changes the color." },
  { id: "chitin", name: "Chitin", formula: "C16H28N2O11", formulaLabel: "C16H28N2O11 piece", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Chitin is the tough repeat in insect shells and in many mushroom walls. This model is two N-acetylglucosamine sugars joined by the beta link.", note: "A shell is this pair repeated. The drawing is a short piece.", world: "A materials scientist meets chitin in insect shells and in many mushroom walls. A close relative, chitosan, shows up in some wound dressings and in water treatment. This model is two sugars of the chain, not the whole shell." },
  { id: "polyethylene", name: "Polyethylene", formula: "C10H22", formulaLabel: "C10H22 piece", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Polyethylene is the plastic in many bags and bottles. This model is ten carbons of the chain, with hydrogen at both ends.", note: "A bag is this pattern repeated thousands of times.", world: "A polymer engineer and a packaging designer use polyethylene in bags, bottles, and pipe. The chain length and the branching change whether the plastic is a soft film or a rigid bottle. This model is ten carbons of that chain, not a whole bag." },
  { id: "pdms", name: "Silicone", formula: "C8H24O2Si3", formulaLabel: "C8H24O2Si3 piece", category: "materials", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "This silicone is PDMS, a flexible chain of silicon–oxygen links with methyl groups. The model is three silicons of that chain.", note: "The ends are capped with methyl groups. A rubber continues the chain.", world: "A biomedical engineer uses this silicone for flexible tubing and for the soft chips in microfluidics, the small channels that move drops of liquid. A cook meets a silicone of the same family in some bakeware. This model is three silicons of the chain, not the whole rubber." },

  // Cosmos & atmosphere
  { id: "water", name: "Water", formula: "H2O", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Water is bent, not straight. The angle at oxygen in this model is 104.5°.", world: "A physician and a nurse start from water: it is the solvent in blood, and an intravenous fluid is built around it. A water-treatment engineer moves it from a river or a well to a tap and tests what is dissolved in it. The bend at oxygen is the shape of that familiar molecule." },
  { id: "carbon-dioxide", name: "Carbon dioxide", formula: "CO2", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Carbon dioxide is a straight line: oxygen, carbon, oxygen. Each carbon–oxygen link is a double bond.", world: "A climate scientist tracks carbon dioxide because burning fuel adds it to the air. An anesthesiologist watches it in a patient's breath. A beverage chemist dissolves it to make a drink fizzy. The molecule is a straight line of oxygen, carbon, oxygen." },
  { id: "ozone", name: "Ozone", formula: "O3", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Ozone is three oxygens in a bent chain. It smells sharp near lightning and, high up, blocks ultraviolet light.", note: "Both bonds are 1.278 Å. A book sometimes draws one single and one double; that is one picture of the same two bonds.", world: "An atmospheric chemist tracks the ozone high in the atmosphere because it blocks ultraviolet light, and tracks ozone near the ground because that same molecule irritates lungs. A water-treatment engineer uses ozone to disinfect some drinking water. The sharp smell near lightning is this molecule." },
  { id: "methane", name: "Methane", formula: "CH4", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Methane is the main gas in natural gas. Carbon sits in the middle of four hydrogens, a tetrahedron.", world: "A refinery engineer and a gas engineer move methane as the main part of natural gas, a fuel for heat and for power plants. A farmer and a climate scientist also meet it as a gas from livestock and from wetlands. Carbon sits in the middle of four hydrogens." },
  { id: "ammonia", name: "Ammonia", formula: "H3N", formulaLabel: "NH3", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Ammonia is a pyramid: nitrogen and three hydrogens. The angle in this model is 106.7°.", world: "A chemical engineer produces ammonia at huge scale for fertilizer, and a farmer spreads that fertilizer. A refrigeration engineer also meets ammonia as a coolant in some industrial systems. The pyramid of nitrogen and three hydrogens is the molecule in both jobs." },
  { id: "silica", name: "Silica", formula: "O2Si", formulaLabel: "SiO2", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Silica is the silicon–oxygen network in sand and quartz. SiO2 is a ratio, not a small gas molecule.", facts: [["What it is", "The silicon–oxygen network in quartz and sand. SiO2 is the ratio of that network."], ["Where it shows up", "Sand, quartz, glass, and a great deal of rock."], ["Why it matters", "Each silicon sits among oxygens, and each oxygen bridges silicons. That is what makes sand hard."]], world: "A glassmaker melts silica sand into glass. A geologist names quartz when the same network has grown as a crystal. A semiconductor engineer starts from silica on the way to silicon for chips. SiO2 is the ratio of that network, not a small gas." },
  { id: "trihydrogen-cation", name: "Trihydrogen cation", formula: "", formulaLabel: "H3+", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "H3+ is a triangle of three hydrogens missing one electron. It is common in space.", facts: [["What it is", "Three hydrogens in a triangle, missing one electron. Written H3+."], ["Where it shows up", "Interstellar clouds, and as an ion in the laboratory."], ["Why it matters", "It is common in space, and a lot of chemistry between the stars starts from it."]], world: "An astrochemist looks for H3+ in space because a lot of the chemistry between the stars starts from this ion. A laboratory spectroscopist studies the same triangle to match signals from telescopes. It is three hydrogens missing one electron." },
  { id: "endohedral-fullerene", name: "Endohedral fullerenes", formula: "", formulaLabel: "M@C2n", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "An endohedral fullerene is a carbon cage with an atom trapped inside, written like La@C82.", facts: [["What it is", "A carbon cage with an atom trapped inside, written like La@C82."], ["Where it shows up", "Made in small amounts. The entry is the family, not one cage."], ["Why it matters", "The trapped atom sits inside a closed shell of carbon. Metal and cage size both change the molecule."]], world: "A materials scientist studies endohedral fullerenes when a metal atom trapped in a carbon cage might be useful, including research on imaging agents. The cage has to stay closed, which is the point of the molecule. The entry is the family, written like La@C82, not one cage." },
  { id: "sulfuric-acid", name: "Sulfuric acid", formula: "H2O4S", formulaLabel: "H2SO4", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Sulfuric acid is the strong acid of school labs and of a great deal of industry.", world: "A chemical engineer treats sulfuric acid as one of the most widely made industrial chemicals, used to make fertilizer and to process metals. A school teacher uses a dilute solution and still treats the concentrated acid as a serious burn hazard. The industrial tank and the lab bottle are the same formula at very different stakes." },
  { id: "iron-oxide", name: "Iron(III) oxide", formula: "Fe2O3", category: "cosmos", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Iron(III) oxide is the red of rust. Fe2O3 counts two irons for every three oxygens.", facts: [["What it is", "The red of rust. Fe2O3 counts two irons for every three oxygens, in a crystal."], ["Where it shows up", "Rust, some iron ores, and the pigment red ochre."], ["Why it matters", "Iron and oxygen pack in a repeating solid. The red color is that crystal."]], world: "A geologist and a miner meet iron(III) oxide as rust and as iron ore. A paint maker uses the red as a pigment. A steelmaker cares about the same crystal because the ore is a feedstock for metal." },

  // Botanical & animal anomalies
  { id: "glycine", name: "Glycine", formula: "C2H5NO2", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Glycine is the smallest amino acid. Its central carbon holds two hydrogens, so it has no mirror image.", note: "This model is the neutral acid. In water the nitrogen is usually charged and the acid has given up its hydrogen.", world: "A biochemist treats glycine as the smallest amino acid, common in proteins such as collagen. A surgeon meets it in some fluids used to rinse the bladder during a prostate procedure. It has no mirror image, because the central carbon holds two hydrogens." },
  { id: "thymine", name: "Thymine", formula: "C5H6N2O2", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Thymine is a DNA base. It pairs with adenine. RNA uses uracil in thymine's place.", world: "A biochemist uses thymine to explain base pairing in DNA: it pairs with adenine. A medicinal chemist who works on nucleoside medicines pays attention to bases like thymine, because a small change in a base can change how a cell copies DNA. RNA uses uracil in thymine's place." },
  { id: "chlorophyll-a", name: "Chlorophyll a", formula: "C55H72MgN4O5", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Chlorophyll a is the green pigment that catches light in plants. Magnesium sits in a large ring.", note: "The long tail is the phytyl chain.", world: "A botanist and a plant physiologist study chlorophyll a because it catches the light a leaf uses. A food chemist watches the green fade when a vegetable is cooked and the color changes. Magnesium sits in the large ring, and the long tail is the phytyl chain." },
  { id: "hemoglobin", name: "Hemoglobin", formula: "", formulaLabel: "protein", category: "bio", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Hemoglobin is the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Iron sits in each heme.", facts: [["What it is", "The protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Four chains, each with a heme and an iron."], ["Where it shows up", "Red blood cells."], ["Why it matters", "Oxygen binds the iron and is let go where a tissue needs it."]], world: "A physician orders a hemoglobin measurement to see whether the blood can carry enough oxygen. A respiratory therapist and an anesthesiologist care about the same protein when a person is short of breath or under anesthesia. Iron in each heme is where the oxygen binds." },
  { id: "resveratrol", name: "Resveratrol", formula: "C14H12O3", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Resveratrol is a molecule in grape skins, studied for what it does in the body.", note: "The link between the rings is trans.", world: "A food chemist finds resveratrol in grape skins and in red wine. Nutrition researchers have studied it. A physician does not treat it as a proven medicine." },
  { id: "curcumin", name: "Curcumin", formula: "C21H20O6", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Curcumin is the yellow molecule in turmeric.", note: "Both links in the chain are trans. This drawing is the diketone.", world: "A cook and a food chemist use curcumin as the yellow color, and part of the flavor, of turmeric. Researchers have studied the molecule widely. A physician still treats turmeric as a food, not as a substitute for a medicine." },
  { id: "luciferin", name: "Luciferin", formula: "C11H8N2O3S2", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", teach: "Firefly luciferin is the molecule a firefly uses when it makes light.", note: "This is D-luciferin, the form a firefly uses.", world: "A biochemist uses firefly luciferin, together with the enzyme luciferase, as a light-producing test in the laboratory. A biologist in the field explains the same reaction when a firefly flashes. This is the form a firefly uses." },
  { id: "tetrodotoxin", name: "Tetrodotoxin", formula: "C11H17N3O8", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Tetrodotoxin is the poison in pufferfish. It blocks channels that nerves need to fire.", world: "Emergency physicians treat poisoning from pufferfish, which contains tetrodotoxin. Neuroscientists have used it in the laboratory as a tool that blocks the channels nerves need to fire. Licensed chefs in Japan are trained to keep the poisonous parts of the fish off the plate." },
  { id: "cantharidin", name: "Cantharidin", formula: "C10H12O4", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Cantharidin is a blistering molecule made by blister beetles.", world: "A dermatologist applies a licensed form of cantharidin to molluscum, a viral skin infection. Toxicologists know the beetle molecule as a blistering poison, which is why that medicine stays with a trained clinician. It is not a home remedy taken from an insect." },
  { id: "charybdotoxin", name: "Charybdotoxin", formula: "", formulaLabel: "peptide", category: "bio", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Charybdotoxin is a peptide in scorpion venom that blocks a potassium channel.", facts: [["What it is", "A peptide in scorpion venom: a short chain of amino acids tied with sulfur bridges."], ["Where it shows up", "Venom of the scorpion Leiurus quinquestriatus."], ["Why it matters", "It blocks a potassium channel that nerves use."]], world: "Physiologists use charybdotoxin in the laboratory as a tool that blocks one kind of potassium channel, which lets them see what that channel does. It comes from the venom of a scorpion. It is a research tool, not a medicine a pharmacy dispenses." },
  { id: "salvinorin-a", name: "Salvinorin A", formula: "C23H28O8", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Salvinorin A is the main molecule in the plant Salvia divinorum.", world: "Neuroscientists study salvinorin A because it acts on a receptor that most plant molecules of this kind do not. It is the main molecule in Salvia divinorum. It is not a medicine on a pharmacy shelf." },
  { id: "digoxin", name: "Digoxin", formula: "C41H64O14", category: "bio", structureMode: "ballstick", restraint: true, teach: "Digoxin is a heart medicine from foxglove. The useful amount and a poisonous amount are different, and the amount is not on this page.", world: "A cardiologist prescribes digoxin for some heart-rhythm problems and for some people with heart failure. A pharmacist and a nurse watch the dose closely, because the useful amount and a poisonous amount are not far apart. The medicine comes from foxglove, and the amount is not on this page." },

  // Future & exotic
  { id: "prithivi", name: "Prithivi", formula: "", formulaLabel: "proposed", category: "future", structureMode: "schematic", teach: "Prithivi is a theoretical name on this list. There is no agreed formula, and there is no bottle of it.", facts: [["What it is", "A theoretical name on this list, with no agreed atoms and no formula."], ["Where it shows up", "Not in a bottle, and not as a structure in a public database."], ["Why it matters", "The list keeps a name that is not a molecule you can count."]], world: "No chemist, engineer, or physician handles prithivi, because there is no agreed formula and no bottle of it. A teacher can use the name to show that a list sometimes contains a word that is not yet a molecule. There is nothing to order from a supplier." },
  { id: "rotaxanes", name: "Rotaxanes", formula: "", formulaLabel: "interlocked rings", category: "future", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "A rotaxane is a ring trapped on a dumbbell-shaped molecule. It cannot slip off unless a bond breaks.", facts: [["What it is", "A ring trapped on a dumbbell-shaped molecule. Stops on the ends keep the ring from sliding off."], ["Where it shows up", "A family of laboratory molecules, not one formula."], ["Why it matters", "The ring can move along the dumbbell. The motion does not require a bond to break."]], world: "A supramolecular chemist builds rotaxanes as molecules in which a ring can slide along a dumbbell. The 2016 Nobel Prize in Chemistry honored work on molecules that can be linked and switched in that spirit. These are laboratory molecules, not parts in a consumer gadget." },
  { id: "dna-origami", name: "DNA origami", formula: "", formulaLabel: "folded DNA", category: "future", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "DNA origami folds a long DNA strand into a designed shape, using short strands as staples.", facts: [["What it is", "A long DNA strand folded into a designed shape and held by short staple strands."], ["Where it shows up", "Nanotechnology laboratories."], ["Why it matters", "The sequence of the staples chooses the fold, so the shape is written in the bases."]], world: "A nanotechnologist folds a long DNA strand into a chosen shape, such as a tile or a box, and holds it with short staple strands. Biomedical researchers study those shapes in experiments about carrying other molecules. The sequence of the staples is the design." },
  { id: "graphyne", name: "Graphyne", formula: "", formulaLabel: "carbon sheet", category: "future", structureMode: "schematic", teach: "Graphyne is a predicted carbon sheet: hexagons joined by two-carbon links that would be triple bonds.", facts: [["What it is", "A predicted carbon sheet: hexagons joined by two-carbon links."], ["Where it shows up", "A prediction. Short pieces have been reported; the endless sheet is the idea."], ["Why it matters", "Those links would be triple bonds, a different sheet from graphene."]], sketch: "graphyne", world: "A materials theorist studies graphyne as a predicted carbon sheet that is not the same as graphene. A synthetic chemist has reported short pieces of that pattern. An engineer does not yet order graphyne the way they order a plastic." },
  { id: "metallic-hydrogen", name: "Metallic hydrogen", formula: "", formulaLabel: "metallic H", category: "future", structureMode: "schematic", teach: "Metallic hydrogen is a form hydrogen can take under extreme pressure, the kind inside a giant planet.", facts: [["What it is", "Hydrogen squeezed until it would conduct like a metal."], ["Where it shows up", "Predicted inside giant planets. A sample on Earth is still disputed."], ["Why it matters", "It would be a metal made from the lightest element."]], world: "A planetary scientist uses the idea of metallic hydrogen to think about the interiors of Jupiter and Saturn. A high-pressure physicist tries to make and identify a sample on Earth, and those claims are still argued over. It would be a metal made from the lightest element." },
  { id: "carbyne", name: "Carbyne", formula: "", formulaLabel: "carbon chain", category: "future", structureMode: "schematic", teach: "Carbyne is a predicted thread of carbon, with single and triple bonds taking turns.", facts: [["What it is", "A predicted thread of carbon, single and triple bonds taking turns."], ["Where it shows up", "A prediction, and a few short chains in the laboratory."], ["Why it matters", "A long thread of that pattern would be an extreme carbon material."]], sketch: "carbyne", world: "A materials theorist studies carbyne as a predicted thread of carbon, single and triple bonds taking turns. A chemist has made short chains of that pattern. Nobody spools it like a fiber in a factory." },
  { id: "borromean-rings", name: "Borromean rings", formula: "", formulaLabel: "three rings", category: "future", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "Stoddart’s Borromean rings are three molecular rings linked so that opening one frees the other two.", facts: [["What it is", "Three molecular rings linked so that opening any one frees the other two."], ["Where it shows up", "Made in the laboratory. The name comes from the Borromeo family crest."], ["Why it matters", "No two rings are linked as a pair. The link needs all three."]], world: "A supramolecular chemist studies molecular Borromean rings as a link that needs all three rings: open one and the other two come apart. The name comes from a family crest. It is a laboratory structure and a lesson in how rings can be held without a bond between each pair." },
  { id: "plutonium-hexafluoride", name: "Plutonium hexafluoride", formula: "F6Pu", formulaLabel: "PuF6", category: "future", structureMode: "card-only", restraint: true, teach: "Plutonium hexafluoride is a radioactive compound written PuF6.", facts: [["What it is", "A radioactive compound written PuF6."], ["Where it shows up", "The literature of nuclear materials, not a classroom bottle."], ["Why it matters", "It is an extreme on this list. The public fact is the formula."]], world: "Nuclear engineers meet plutonium compounds only inside regulated plants. PuF6 belongs to that specialized literature, not to a classroom bottle. The public fact is the formula and the radioactivity." },
  { id: "helium-hydride", name: "Helium hydride ion", formula: "", formulaLabel: "HeH+", category: "future", structureMode: "card-only", teach: "HeH+ is a real ion of helium and hydrogen, found in space and tied to the early universe in these lessons.", facts: [["What it is", "An ion of one helium and one hydrogen, HeH+."], ["Where it shows up", "Space, including a planetary nebula."], ["Why it matters", "These lessons tie it to the early universe: helium bonded to hydrogen."]], world: "An astrochemist looks for HeH+ when the question is which molecules formed early. It has been detected in a planetary nebula. The ion is a research subject, not a material anyone stores." },
  { id: "n8", name: "N8", formula: "N8", category: "future", structureMode: "schematic", restraint: true, teach: "N8 is a predicted molecule made only of nitrogen. It is not a stable bottle on a shelf.", facts: [["What it is", "A predicted molecule that would contain only nitrogen."], ["Where it shows up", "Calculations, not a bottle on a shelf."], ["Why it matters", "Ordinary nitrogen gas is N2. N8 would be a different arrangement."]], world: "No one handles a bottle of N8, because it is a prediction rather than a product. Energetic-materials researchers talk about all-nitrogen molecules as an idea. Ordinary nitrogen gas is N2, and this page stops at that public fact." },
];
